Date: Thu, 16 Dec 93 10:35:15 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Extropian children, birth

Jay Freeman and James Baker dialog on biological causes and sources
of the difficulty of human birth:

> [...]

Some animals have young that are relatively helpless at birth (nomenclature:
"altricial") -- they require substantial postnatal development before they
can function effectively on their own.  Some others have young that are
born ready-to-go, batteries included (nomenclature: "precocial").  In one
class I took, the professor stated that one evolutionary pressure for
altrical young was that the effort -- food, energy, time, risk and so forth --
provided by the parents in support of the young was more nearly incremental
in the case of altricial young than in the case of precocial young, so that
there was less to be lost if the process was interrupted for some reason.
He said it was like the difference between pay-in-advance and pay-as-you-go.
Another, somewhat related, was that with altricial young, reproductive rate
can track conditions in real time at less cost that with precocial young;
thus if there's not enough food to feed all the baby birds, it's more
advantageous to the species if the ones that die did not have all the
"effort" that it takes to make precocial young already invested in them.
In the case of live-bearing animals, the mother is hampered by carrying
embryos less if they are born before development is complete (Chip, imagine
what it would be like if your wife had born a teenager!).  And if parental
care is of use in furthering reproductive success, then the altricial
strategy may permit both parents to start providing it sooner and more
effectively than the precocial one.

On the other hand, if animals cannot make safe nests (or whatever) for
altricial young, the juveniles become lunch for predators.  Thus it is no
surprise that ground-nesting birds generally have precocial young, whereas
tree-nesters generally have altricial young.  Rabbits and hares are closely
related, but the former have altricial young, raised in burrows, whereas
hares live in areas where safe burrows are not practical, and have precocial
young.

Thus I suspect that the fact that human infants are altricial may in great
part reflect our arboreal ancestry, and also perhaps a developing social
structure that provided group security once we had become ground-dwellers.

                                              -- Jay Freeman

