Date: Thu, 16 Dec 1993 11:01:27 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: List Justice--Why It's Likely to be Flawed

As one who has also made snide remarks about the List Justice (tm)
system, and as one who is not in the Inner Circle of the ExI, let me
defend the ExI justice system, or, rather, argue that what we have is
pretty much to be expected.

Harvey Newstrom wrote:

> Harry Hawk quotes Clark Reynard as saying:
> >> That is the last I heard about the matter; I still have
> >> never received any more information about what I did,
> >> or why I was removed from the list, and had no reason
> >> to care until recently.
> 
> This reminds me of the previous poster who was prevented from posting based 
> on an agreement that he claimed he didn't make.  I still keep having these 
> nagging feelings that List Justice(tm) is actually based on arbitrary 
> judgements passed out with no chance of recourse, where appeals are ignored 
> and complaints are censored from the list.  If this person was indeed 
> removed from the list (or prevented from posting/joining/rejoining) in any 
> way, shouldn't this decision have been announced, and shouldn't the person 
> been given a chance to seek arbitration from other list members?

Having watched the machinations of our legal system for a year and a
half now, I've concluded a few things that help to explain the
apparently capricious nature of the justice system:

1. The justice system is very rarely invoked, no more than once or
twice a month--at least that the "public" sees (I'm not sure what
charges and rulings are happening outside our view).

2. Not being a regular event, thankfully, the "machinery" is not
"oiled." No helpful bureaucracy to submit paperwork, check claims,
ensure that all points of law have been raised, etc. 

3. Thus, everyone is acting on pretty much an ad hoc basis. No
preparation of legal briefs, no formal argument before a judge, no law
clerks to study case law that may be relevant, etc.

4. Many semantic quibbles surface, as people claim their words were
taken the wrong way, that they were being ironic or facetious, that
they were misquoted, etc.

5. Harry the Judge is thus burdened with having to do what lawyers,
clerks, and secretaries would do in the Real World (tm). 

6. The Appeals Court, Andrea Gallagher, has a low visibility on the
List and may not even be following the List at all. No slam on Andrea,
but she can hardly evaluate the "justice" of decisions if she is not
an active participant in the debate. (This is my contention, that
Justice does not come from a formalistic set of rules but from a kind
of market approach....there are many points one may dispute here, of
course.)

7. Every time a major ruling is handed out--someone is suspended,
kicked off, found innocent, etc.--there are calls for discussion (like
this one!) and calls that we alter the system in major ways. Usually
Harry will reiterate his support for "subcontractors" and PPL
experiments (wherein some group agrees to be bound by decisions of a
mediator or judge). Then the whole issue gets dropped for another
couple of months, until the next time.

My basic point is this: the List Justice system is invoked too
infrequently to be running smoothly. Further, the burden is placed on
Harry (or whoever judges) to do the legwork that would normally fall
on the lawyers, clerks, and secretaries in the real world.

There are several possible fixes:

* Institute a "filing fee" for List Justice claims. Someone who feels
wronged and who wants justice should not get the services of Harry or
anyone else for free. What should this fee be? Let's debate it.

(Better yet, let Harry declare what his fee is. The issue is clouded
by the fact that the ExI/List has effectively granted him a kind of
monopoly, so his fee may not be a purely free market thing, and part
or all of it may need to go to the ExI rather than into his pocket.)

* Absent such a fee system, the "tragedy of the commons" I
demonstrated a few months back (when I filed against several people
for minor violations of the letter of the law) will continue to occur.
The List Justice system is a "commons" that is being "over-grazed" by
spiteful or thin-skinned folks, and perhaps "under-grazed" by those
with some legitimate gripes who are turned-off by the slow process and
often capricious nature of the judgements.

* The "jury" idea. Each quarter pick from the List a panel of 3
jurors. Lots of details to debate! (Volunteers or mandatory jury pool?
Active posters or lurkers included? Etc.)

Well, that's it.

I have little expectation that this proposal will go any further than
any of my previous proposals or those of others. The issue will fade
out until the Next Big Flap, when there'll be a flurry of "the justice
system is broken" posts and much hand-wringing. Then the issue will
fade again....

"If it ain't broke, don't fix it." Does this mean that since we never
fix it, it ain't really broke?

--Tim May


