Extropians:

I'd like to make a few comments about some areas where others may be
confused or in error concerning my last post.

Required Safety Course
----------------------

It is my understanding from the newspaper and gun shop employees that
as of last July, all gun purchasers are technically required to show
evidence of completion of a standard safety course.  Regulations
require this course to be simple, cheap, and easily available to
everyone.  I've been told subsequently that the government does not
currently have the money or interest to enforce this requirement.

Security of Ruger mini-14
-------------------------

The Feinstein rider does not mention specific weapon makes or models
(as does the CA law), but designates prohibited weapons first by
class: e.g. any Colt AR-15 series or copy, Kalishnikov AK series or
copy, Intertec TEC-DC series or copy, etc...  Currently, weapons
manufacturers are playing games with semantics by marketing copies of
prohibited or soon-to-be prohibited models.  The Feinstein rider would
eliminate this loophole.

Secondly, the rider designates prohibited weapons by combination of
attributes, such as some number of the following:  semi-auto
operation, pistol grip, flash supressor, barrel type, > 10 round
clips, etc...  The Ruger rifles (.223 mini-14 and 7.62x39mm mini-30)
do not meet all the requirements of this list primarily because they
do have have bayonette mounts or pistol grips.  They do meet most of
the other requirements, so it's really only a matter of a technicality
or two.

My understanding is that the Ruger weapons continue to survive
legislation primarily because the company has maintained a formidable
lobbying presense in Washington, while Colt has fallen out of favor.
I would not feel very secure in the immunity from government tampering
of an investment in one of these weapons, as this luxury could change
as fast as the political climate.  Once the Colt .223 is banned, the
mini-14 won't be far behind. 

Shotguns
--------

I have not recommended shotguns because I know very little about them.
I do understand that they can be quite effective, and should be
considered for home defense.

Revolvers vs. Autos
-------------------

I do not recommend a revolver over an automatic pistol.  I do,
however, recommend a revolver over an auto for a beginning shooter, or
for someone who will not devote much time to proper gun maintainance
or target practice.  I do recommend an auto, especially a Glock or
Sig, to anyone who is willing to devote that time and effort.

Caliber/ammo Effectiveness
--------------------------

Based on the response to my comments on caliber efficiency, it seems
that some people may be confused (or just plain wrong) about handgun
ballistics.  The following is summarized from the "1993 Winchester
Ammunition Product Guide":

Load                      Weight (grains)  Velocity (fps)  Energy (ft-lbs)
----                      ---------------  --------------  ---------------
9mm Luger full jacket     115              1155            341
9mm Luger Black Talon     147              990             320

.40 S&W full jacket       155              1125            436
.40 S&W Black Talon       180              990             392

10mm full jacket          155              1125            436
10mm Black Talon          200              990             435

.45 ACP full jacket       230              835             356
.45 ACP Black Talon       230              850             369

.357 magnum semi jacket   110              1295            410
.357 magnum semi jacket   158              1235            535
.357 magnum Black Talon   180              1180            557

.44 magnum semi jacket    240              1180            741
.44 magnum Black Talon    250              1330            982

.45 Colt lead ball        255              860             420
.45 Colt hollow point     225              920             423

Some people judge ammo effectiveness based on the size of the bullet
hole, some base it on muzzle velocity, and some base it on muzzle
energy.  Any way you stack it, it seems clear that:

  1.  The 9mm and .45 automatic are almost equivalent.  Depending on
      which parameter you prefer, the .45 is slightly worse than the 9
      (slower) or slightly better than the 9 (heavier and more energy).
  2.  The .40 and 10mm are almost equivalent, and represent the best
      of the auto calibers.
  3.  The .45 revolver shoots faster and harder than the .45 auto, but
      not by much.  In principle, the main issue is barrel length.  Longer
      barrels fire faster bullets.  That's why rifles can typically launch
      projectiles at over 3000 fps, while handgun velocities are typically
      1000 fps.
  4.  The best 10mm auto loads are roughly equivalent to low-end .357
      magnum revolver loads.  However, good .357 cartridges release more
      than 25% more energy than 10mm.

In addition to these statistics, the following are summarized from the
Sept '93 issue of "Handguns".  These figures represent the results of
different ammo fired on live subjects (goats) under controled
conditions.  I summarize the "best" and "typical" results for Average
Incapacitation Time (the time it took for the goat to fall over after
being shot) for several calibers:

Load                      Weight (grains)  Energy (ft-lbs)  AIT (sec)
----                      ---------------  ---------------  ---------
9mm MagSafe +P            68               461              4.74
9mm Win Black Talon       147              302              9.68
9mm Win full jacket       115              345              14.4

.40 MagSafe               84               572              4.52
.40 Win Black Talon       180              391              8.86
.40 Win full jacket       155              430              13.76

10mm MagSafe              96               637              4.48
10mm Win Black Talon      200              432              8.76
10mm Win full jacket      155              419              13.98

.45 ACP MagSafe +P        96               576              4.68
.45 ACP Black Talon       230              351              9.14
.45 ACP Fed full jacket   230              360              13.84

.357 magnum QuickShock    125              551              4.40
.357 magnum Win semi      110              401              7.98
.357 magnum Win semi      158              545              8.34

One can observe from this table that:

  1.  The goat died from each of these shots.  (Actually, I think
      there were some small-caliber cases not shown where the goat had
      to be shot more than once.)  Therefore each of these calibers is
      effective as a lethal weapon.
  2.  The "specialty" loads were most effective, and were about
      equally effective in all calibers.  Loads such as Glasers and
      MagSafes are shot or "pre-fragmented" payloads, not solid bullets.
  3.  In the auto calibers, the hollowpoints were roughly equally
      effective.  As expected, the .40 and 10mm were slightly more effective
      than the 9 or .45.
  4.  "Standard" lead-core/full-jacket loads were considerably less
      effective than hollowpoints in all calibers, especially 9mm.
  5.  In all cases, the .357 magnum outperformed all auto calibers.
      Furthermore, the "standard" .357 load was as good as or better than
      most hollowpoint auto loads.

It seems relatively clear that on a per-shot basis, the magnum
revolver calibers consistently outperform the autos.  The primary
issue is whether the 3x capacity of the auto (18 shots in Glock
9mm vs. 6 in S&W .357) is worth the fact that you have to use twice
as many shots.


